To connect a website to a Firebase database, you need to follow these steps:

1[1].
Set up a Firebase project: Sign up for a Firebase account at https://firebase.google.com/[2].
Create a new project by clicking on the "Add project" button[3].
Fill in the required information and click on "Create Project.[4]"
Add Firebase to your web app: In your Firebase project, click on the gear icon in the top left corner and navigate to "Project Settings.[5]"
Under the "General" tab, scroll down to find the "Your apps" section and click the "</>" icon to add a web app[6].
Enter your app's name and click "Register app"[7].
You'll be given a piece of code with your Firebase config object[8].
Copy this code[9].
Install Firebase SDK: You can either use the CDN or install the Firebase via npm[10].
To use the CDN, add the following script tags to your HTML file's head section:

```html
<!-- Firebase App (the core Firebase SDK) -->
<script src="https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/8.0.0/firebase-app.js"></script>

<!-- Add Firebase products that you want to use -->
<script src="https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/8.0.0/firebase-firestore.js"></script>
```

If you prefer to use npm, run the following command:

```bash
npm install --save firebase
```

And then, import and initialize Firebase in your JavaScript file:

```javascript
import * as firebase from "firebase/app";
import "firebase/firestore";
```

4[11].
Initialize Firebase: Paste the copied Firebase config object in your JavaScript file and use it to initialize the Firebase app[12].
```javascript
// Your web app's Firebase configuration
var firebaseConfig = {
  apiKey: "your-api-key",
  authDomain: "your-auth-domain",
  projectId: "your-project-id",
  storageBucket: "your-storage-bucket",
  messagingSenderId: "your-sender-id",
  appId: "your-app-id",
};

// Initialize Firebase
firebase.initializeApp(firebaseConfig);

// Get a reference to the Firestore service
const db = firebase.firestore();
```

5[13].
Interact with the Firebase database: Now you can read, write, and update data in your Firebase database from your web app[14].
Here's an example of adding data to a collection:

```javascript
// Add a new document with a generated ID
db.collection("users").add({
  first_name: "Ada",
  last_name: "Lovelace"
})
  .then((docRef) => {
    console.log("Document written with ID: ", docRef.id);
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error("Error adding document: ", error);
  });
```

For more examples and documentation on how to interact with the Firebase database, refer to the official Firestore documentation .

To create a floating label for your input fields in a web application, you can use HTML, CSS, and JavaScript or a CSS framework like Bootstrap or Material-UI  [1].
Here's a simple implementation using HTML and CSS:

HTML:
```html
<div class="floating-label">
  <input type="text" id="input-text" class="floating-input" placeholder=" " required>
  <label for="input-text" class="floating-label">Your Label</label>
</div>
```

CSS:
```css
.floating-label {
  position: relative;
}

.floating-input,
.floating-label {
  font-size: 16px;
  line-height: 24px;
}

.floating-input {
  display: block;
  padding: 8px 0;
  border: none;
  border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc;
  outline: none;
  transition: border-color 0.3s ease;
}

.floating-label {
  position: absolute;
  top: 16px;
  left: 0;
  pointer-events: none;
  color: #9b9b9b;
  transition: 0.3s ease top, 0.3s ease color;
}

.floating-input:focus,
.floating-input:not(:placeholder-shown) {
  border-color: #000;
}

.floating-input:focus + .floating-label,
.floating-input:not(:placeholder-shown) + .floating-label {
  top: -8px;
  color: #000;
}
```

This code creates a floating label effect by positioning the label absolutely within a relative container [2].
When the input field is focused or has content, the label moves to the top and changes color [3].
The transition property provides a smooth animation effect as the label transitions to the focused state [4].

