Mercerizing a fabric before desizing, before bleaching, and after bleaching has its own advantages and disadvantages.

Mercerizing before desizing: 
Advantages: Improve fabric strength, enhanced luster, increased dye affinity; Disadvantages: Limited or no impact on desizing, low degree of mercerization obtained, difficult to recover sodium hydroxide [2];

Mercerizing before bleaching: 
Advantages: Improved bleach absorption and increased whiteness, enhanced luster, increased dye affinity;
Disadvantages: can reduce fabric strength, difficult to recover sodium hydroxide;

Mercerizing after bleaching: 
Advantages: the highest degree of mercerization is obtained, improve fabric luster, absorbency, and dye affinity;
Disadvantages: Reduced whiteness, additional processing steps (washing, neutralization and drying).
Advantages of Grey Mercerization (before desizing): Improve fabric strength, enhanced luster, increased dye affinity.
One drying is avoided, as in other cases, drying is required before Mercerization, saving energy and processing time [2]. However, the impurities present in cotton dissolved in caustic makes sodium hydroxide recovery more difficult and costly.

